### Implementation

#### 1. Custom `ChatClient` with OAuth 2.0
Create a custom `ChatClient` implementation that uses an OAuth 2.0 token via `azure-identity`.

```java
package com.example.dice;

import com.azure.ai.openai.OpenAIClient;
import com.azure.ai.openai.OpenAIClientBuilder;
import com.azure.ai.openai.models.ChatCompletionsOptions;
import com.azure.ai.openai.models.ChatRequestMessage;
import com.azure.core.credential.TokenCredential;
import com.azure.identity.ClientSecretCredentialBuilder;
import org.springframework.ai.chat.ChatClient;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Value;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;

import java.util.Collections;

@Configuration
public class AzureOpenAIConfig {

    @Value("${azure.openai.endpoint}")
    private String endpoint;

    @Value("${azure.openai.deployment-name}")
    private String deploymentName;

    @Value("${azure.client-id}")
    private String clientId;

    @Value("${azure.client-secret}")
    private String clientSecret;

    @Value("${azure.tenant-id}")
    private String tenantId;

    @Bean
    public ChatClient chatClient() {
        // Create TokenCredential using ClientSecretCredential (or other credential types)
        TokenCredential credential = new ClientSecretCredentialBuilder()
            .clientId(clientId)
            .clientSecret(clientSecret)
            .tenantId(tenantId)
            .build();

        // Build Azure OpenAI client with OAuth 2.0 token
        OpenAIClient azureOpenAIClient = new OpenAIClientBuilder()
            .endpoint(endpoint)
            .credential(credential)
            .buildClient();

        return new OAuthAzureOpenAIChatClient(azureOpenAIClient, deploymentName);
    }
}

class OAuthAzureOpenAIChatClient implements ChatClient {
    private final OpenAIClient azureOpenAIClient;
    private final String deploymentName;

    public OAuthAzureOpenAIChatClient(OpenAIClient azureOpenAIClient, String deploymentName) {
        this.azureOpenAIClient = azureOpenAIClient;
        this.deploymentName = deploymentName;
    }

    @Override
    public String call(String prompt) {
        ChatCompletionsOptions options = new ChatCompletionsOptions(
            Collections.singletonList(new ChatRequestMessage("user", prompt))
        );
        options.setModel(deploymentName);
        return azureOpenAIClient
            .getChatCompletions(deploymentName, options)
            .getChoices()
            .get(0)
            .getMessage()
            .getContent();
    }
}
```

- **Explanation**:
  - `ClientSecretCredentialBuilder` fetches an OAuth 2.0 token from Azure AD using client credentials flow. You could also use `DefaultAzureCredential` for broader authentication options (e.g., managed identity, interactive login).
  - `OpenAIClientBuilder` uses the `TokenCredential` to authenticate with Azure OpenAI.
  - `OAuthAzureOpenAIChatClient` implements `ChatClient` and delegates to the `azureOpenAIClient`.

